WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.860
- Steering wheel's moving.
Seat belt's fastened.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.550
No one's behind the wheel.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:09.600
Robotaxis are real, at least
here in Chandler, Arizona,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.230
where Waymo has been offering
fully driverless rides

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.720
for more than a year.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.150
Waymo's looking left, Waymo's
looking right. All right.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.770
And in August it expanded
to San Francisco,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.760
but rides here still have
a driver behind the wheel.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.330
The supporting driver does
have hands on the wheel,

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.230
not always gripping the steering wheel,

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.070
but always hovering around the wheel.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.670
It's an important test for the company,

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.850
as it could provide a
roadmap for expansion

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.200
into other cities, but the race is on.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.110
General Motors Cruise is
also testing driverless cabs

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.390
in San Francisco.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.460
- You could really build a
sizable ride-hailing business

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.070
if you set up operations here.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.450
- But rising costs and tech limitations

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.970
have slowed driverless car companies down.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.490
So can Waymo, with operations in Phoenix

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.250
and now in San Francisco,

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.490
pick up the pace and bring its robocabs

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.470
to more cities and more passengers?

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.020
To find out, we spoke to company leaders,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.969
and yes, took a couple of rides.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:10.552
(bright music)

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.040
We're here at Waymo's
depot in San Francisco,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.710
a city that represents an
important test for the company

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.910
as it looks to expand
its service, and well,

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.650
it's got a lot of cars.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.740
Waymo's ride-hailing service, Waymo One,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.040
is new in San Francisco, but the company,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.420
formerly known as Google's
self-driving car project,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.140
has been testing here
for more than a decade.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.700
- San Francisco provides

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.260
a wide diversity of not only geographies,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.020
but also a diversity of
the kind of interactions

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.100
that we'll have on the road itself.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.400
- So you can see, we're in a
little bit of a busier part

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.490
of the neighborhood here.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.710
There are cars parked along the right.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.420
There's a truck here, stopped on loading.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.390
Waymo rides in San Francisco, like the one

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.460
the company invited "The
Wall Street Journal" to take,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.750
are currently done with
a driver at the wheel.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.410
But the company says it's
the tech aboard its vehicles

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.590
that are making the
majority of the decisions.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.660
The tech that I can actually see here,

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.440
what are the main components?

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.670
- The LiDAR, which is the
360 degree at the top,

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.150
while the smaller ones are on the side,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.330
the perimeter LiDAR.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.900
We have cameras, 29 different cameras,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.370
positioned throughout the vehicle,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.150
and radar is a third kind.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.970
And then lastly, within the trunk

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.660
is where we have the majority
of our compute systems,

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.280
so the systems that take
all those signals together

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.030
and combine it, run it
through our software,

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.330
to help the car determine
what it should do next.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.700
- All of Waymo's passengers,
or Trusted Testers,

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.360
in San Francisco have signed
nondisclosure agreements

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.190
that prevent them from
sharing their experience.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:37.683
Well, except for me.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.080
So right now, Waymo's driving us around

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.210
a pretty residential area.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:49.110
Lots of stop signs. No
stoplights on the route so far.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.120
I think seeing the
supporting driver's hands

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.680
hovering and sometimes
grabbing the wheel itself

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.923
is providing a little bit of
additional security for me.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.600
Waymo really likes to
stick to the speed limit.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.890
Last week, a Waymo car hit a
pedestrian in San Francisco.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.600
The company said the
vehicle was being driven

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.580
by a human driver in manual mode

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:11.930
when it struck the pedestrian,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.670
who was treated for injuries at the scene

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.370
and transported to a
hospital by ambulance.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.100
The company said, "The trust
and safety of the communities

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.190
in which we drive are paramount to us,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.400
and we will continue
investigating this incident

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.770
in partnership with local authorities."

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.580
So far, seven companies,
including Waymo and Cruise,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.880
have received permits from the State

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.540
to test autonomous
vehicles without a driver.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.210
But Waymo wants to be the first

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.140
to offer its service to the public.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.950
How far off do you think
you are from being able to

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:41.640
pull the autonomous
specialists from the vehicle?

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.500
- Our current focus is
really continuing to develop

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.410
a safe and convenient experience
for all of our riders,

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.830
so no specific timelines
to share at this time.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.870
- [Adam] Success in San
Francisco could help provide

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.980
a revenue-generating business for Waymo.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.910
Analysts estimate its cars cost $200,000

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.950
and it can't yet charge
passengers for rides in the city.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.570
- They don't need to
operate a robotaxi fleet

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:06.700
in a ton of markets

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.400
because if you look at
the business performance

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.800
and results of Uber and
Lyft, they mostly come from

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.780
just a handful of cities around
the world, like 10 cities.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.280
- [Adam] WSJ reporter Tripp
Mickle covers Alphabet.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.190
He lives in San Francisco and,
like many residents there,

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.610
has been seeing Waymo's cars a lot.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.780
- They seem to be on a rotation or a loop

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.750
in terms of what routes they drive.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.160
- To see what Waymo could
look like in future markets,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.100
we went to Waymo's first location,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.140
a 50-square-mile area
around Chandler, Arizona.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.560
Here, Waymo has pulled the driver support

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.480
and riders can now hail
robotaxis themselves.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.233
The wheel is moving by itself.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.480
When did you know it was
time to pull the driver

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.020
out of the Waymo here in Chandler?

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.040
- We evaluate the
performance of the driver,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.750
the Waymo driver, on a
given operation domain.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.560
We evaluate it against general
features of topography,

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.720
geography, weather, and
also specific features

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.050
such as the unprotected left turns

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.630
and ability to handle a large parking lot.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.630
That is the process that we
used to release our technology

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:10.509
here in Chandler.

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.150
(bright music)

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.340
- After traveling more than
65,000 miles around Chandler

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.940
without a human behind
the wheel, Waymo opened up

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.570
its ride-hailing services
to passengers like me,

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:21.930
willing to try.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.030
While there, I spent about an
hour in the back of a Waymo.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.030
We're turning. (laughs) There it goes.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.970
I took three separate rides,
including one in the dark.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.650
Waymo encountered construction,

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.990
an emergency vehicle,
(siren wailing)

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.400
and took three rights to avoid a left,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.530
though I did experience
one unprotected left turn.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.780
Waiting for intersection to clear,

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.150
and it is starting and stopping a lot.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.523
Okay. You may be good now, Waymo.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.331
Ooh. (exhales) All right, we did it.

00:05:55.331 --> 00:05:56.164
- [Waymo] Arriving shortly.

00:05:56.164 --> 00:05:58.090
- [Adam] Waymo was a defensive driver,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.350
but the experience took
some getting used to.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.980
The biggest hurdle, trusting the tech.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.290
- Trust needs to be earned

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.230
and we earn it by our
excellent track record.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.160
And we also earn it through transparency

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.280
and really engaging with the public.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.950
- [Adam] Before opening up
its ride-hailing service

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:14.840
to the public,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.700
Waymo published safety data
from its tests around Chandler.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.340
It reported 18 collisions

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.010
over a span of about 6
million miles driven.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.210
- None of those collisions
were serious in nature,

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.620
but regardless, every single
event is a learning experience.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.810
- Do you have any updated data

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:33.240
since launching the ride-hailing
service here in Chandler?

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.720
- And I'll tell you that the
positive trends that we saw

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.360
in that first report, where
we evaluated 6 million miles,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.210
has continued through our service.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.170
- [Adam] The company says it
completes hundreds of rides

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.790
in the Chandler area each week

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.240
and has served tens of
thousands of riders there.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.560
- It's put them at the forefront

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.370
of the driverless car effort,

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.100
but it's also Chandler, Arizona.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.100
It's not, you know, in 50
states, or much less two states.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.630
It's really in one, you
know, one suburb of Phoenix.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.980
- Waymo's ambitions, however, are greater.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.960
It's now testing in 25
cities across the US,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.270
though the company wouldn't say

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.363
where it plans to offer rides next.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.410
What's slowing Waymo down

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:17.660
from expanding to cities across the US?

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.610
- It's technology, really.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.300
There's been a realization, broadly,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.690
across the self-driving industry

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.910
that their ambitions and hopes of creating

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.050
something that was like a computer model

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.750
that can be scaled and
dropped anywhere, in any city,

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.150
is not totally realistic.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:37.990
- It took Waymo three years
of on-the-streets testing

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.740
before it offered its ride-hailing service

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.530
in the Chandler area alone.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:43.680
What can we draw from that pace

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.650
for Waymo's future expansion?

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.090
- I don't know if that's a great proxy.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:49.460
We build on our experience
and our technology

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:51.640
such that it takes us less and less time

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.520
to learn the intricacies
and be able to drive safely

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:55.990
and reliably in any market.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.030
- Because of this, Waymo
expects to pick up the pace,

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:00.180
and it might need to.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.540
Cruise has raised more than $9 billion

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.480
to support its robotaxi efforts.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.240
Waymo has raised nearly 6 billion.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.300
But just how many riders
will hop in the back

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.163
of one of these robotaxis,
remains to be seen.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.572
Ride done.

00:08:16.572 --> 00:08:18.006
- [Waymo] We've arrived.

00:08:18.006 --> 00:08:20.589
(bright music)

